### Description

If a `session.webRequest.onHeadersReceived` (or `onBeforeSendHeaders`) listener calls `callback()` with a `responseHeaders` / `requestHeaders` object containing an invalid name or value (e.g. a value with `\r\n` in it), the JS-to-`HttpResponseHeaders` conversion fails partway, but we ignore the converter's return value and apply the empty or partially-built set anyway. The net effect is that the response's original headers get replaced with whatever was converted before the bad entry, with no error surfaced to the app.

Relevant code: `WebRequest::OnHeadersReceivedListenerResult` and `WebRequest::OnBeforeSendHeadersListenerResult` in `shell/browser/api/electron_api_web_request.cc` both discard the `bool` from `gin::Converter<...>::FromV8`.

### Expected behavior

A conversion failure should throw back to the listener (or at minimum leave the original headers untouched and log), not swap in a half-built set.

### Actual behavior

Original headers are silently replaced by the partial override; headers listed after the invalid entry in the object never make it into the response.

### Testcase

```js
session.defaultSession.webRequest.onHeadersReceived((details, callback) => {
  callback({
    responseHeaders: {
      'X-Bad': ['oops\r\nX-Injected: 1'],
      ...details.responseHeaders
    }
  });
});
```

Load any page whose response carries an identifiable header and observe that header is gone.
